# Agent Notes
[pattern] rmcp 2.2.0 exposes per-request cancellation through `RequestContext.ct`; `RunningService::waiting` returns `QuitReason::Closed` on stdio EOF.
[gotcha] rmcp 2.2.0 drains handlers for up to 5 seconds on EOF without cancelling their request tokens; a transport wrapper must signal application shutdown as soon as `receive` returns `None`.
[gotcha] Parallel tracing capture tests need both serialization and a stable global callsite-interest subscriber; per-future dispatch alone can race callsite interest rebuilds under default-thread cargo test.
[pattern] rmcp 2.2.0 `schema_for_input`/`schema_for_output` emits JSON Schema 2020-12 and strips only root title/description; `serde(deny_unknown_fields)` produces `additionalProperties: false`.
[gotcha] Schemars 1.2 emits documented string enums as `oneOf` constant branches and bounded `Option<Vec<T>>` as `type: ["array", "null"]`; strict validation must distinguish those finite/nullable shapes from open untagged unions.
[pattern] anytype-api resolver ambiguity errors carry deterministic wire-safe `ResolveCandidate` values capped at 10 and use a hard scan limit; MCP adapters revalidate them, retain valid alternatives, and refuse empty candidate sets.
[invariant] Resolver duplicate normalization selects a wire-safe representative before lexical ordering; unsafe same-ID rows must never displace a safe ambiguity candidate.
[gotcha] Cursor token parsing must validate ASCII grammar before decoding; byte-index slicing an arbitrary UTF-8 token creates a remotely triggerable panic even when total byte length is bounded.
[pattern] Resolver scans use an explicit 1,000-row policy: unique/not-found requires complete bounded evidence, ambiguity retains a deterministic safe top ten, and view resolution scans for a later exact ID before classifying names.
[gotcha] Handler-side pagination/projection does not bound upstream allocation when the HTTP client first calls `response.bytes().await`; enforce response-byte ceilings while reading the Anytype JSON body.
[pattern] Body-bearing object create/get/delete/update and template-get responses use the document JSON ceiling; lists/search/views use the generic ceiling, errors and raw files have separate policies, and HEAD/304 representation lengths do not imply buffered bodies.
[pattern] MCP continuation offsets must advance from validated upstream pagination offset+limit, not returned item count; sparse pages otherwise repeat data.
[invariant] MCP page completion must verify upstream offset and limit exactly match the request and returned count does not exceed the requested limit before issuing any continuation cursor.
[pattern] Runtime operation diagnostics must enclose upstream calls, handler conversion, and typed result encoding; conversion/encoding failures use static categories and never log error values.
[pattern] Object discovery uses explicit SummaryOnly/Selected/AllBounded projection modes; AllBounded fails rather than truncating, while cursor projection fingerprints sort/dedup keys even though selected output preserves first-request order.
[gotcha] `anytype-api` pagination query serialization omits an explicit zero offset; HTTP path tests should assert the limit and path while treating absent offset as offset zero.
[invariant] Treat unique resolver outputs as untrusted upstream values: validate resolved IDs against the MCP wire/path grammar before passing them to API builders; view builders also reject unsafe selected path segments before URL construction.
[invariant] Object-search handlers execute one explicit upstream page and drop bodies/snippets even if an upstream list unexpectedly returns them; object-get verifies returned object/space identity before projecting or chunking.
[gotcha] Global object search cannot resolve type names or ids without a space, so global `types` values are treated as keys; space-scoped searches resolve all type references through the bounded API resolver.
[invariant] Omitted MCP input fields may select defaults, but explicit JSON null is malformed; use the non-serializable Omittable<T> input wrapper so runtime decoding and generated schemas cannot broaden scope or projection through Option/null semantics.
[invariant] Resolver outputs cross an upstream trust boundary and must be revalidated as bounded domain values before cursor fingerprinting or request construction, even when the resolver accepts explicit fast paths such as `@key`.
[invariant] Mutation handlers must apply MutationAccess before resolver/upstream work; object_archive uses only one object(...).delete_once(), validates safe preflight/evidence identity, and never treats the DELETE body as success evidence.
[gotcha] A single-variant Rust enum becomes a oneOf schema with one branch, which strict_wire_schema rejects; represent invariant singleton output as a scalar const wrapper instead.
[invariant] MutationProgress is one-way and per invocation: mark it immediately before the first write future poll; controlled failures after that point require reread-before-retry conflict guidance, while ordinary returned errors stay handler-classified.
[pattern] Semantic mutation verification uses verify_semantic with both timeout and a validated finite attempt cap; stale fetched values and transient error text must never be retained or logged.
[pattern] Anytype document resource URIs are already canonical path-safe strings: reject all normalization and percent escapes, parse the exact `anytype://spaces/{space_id}/objects/{object_id}` shape before I/O, and rederive the returned URI from validated object/space IDs before accepting content.
[invariant] MCP document resources return a complete current body or an error: enforce both the Anytype document-response byte ceiling and the 100,000-Unicode-character resource cap under the shared operation boundary, never truncate, and keep metadata-rich rmcp Resource links separate from body/properties.
[invariant] Anytype automatic HTTP retries are method-gated before every 429, status, and transport path: POST/PATCH are sent once, while only the established GET/HEAD/PUT/DELETE/OPTIONS set is replay-safe. Mutation handlers must treat non-allowlisted post-dispatch failures as indeterminate.
[invariant] `HttpClient::new` overrides reqwest redirect and retry policies with `none`/`never`; the explicit method-aware middleware loop must remain the sole replay authority so hidden sends cannot bypass metrics, mutation state, or credential boundaries.
[invariant] Explicit full type-ID resolution uses `TypeRequest::get_direct`: one cache-independent scoped GET with exact returned type-ID verification. Do not route bounded protocol lookups through cache-priming `TypeRequest::get`.
[invariant] Bounded direct property metadata paths use `PropertyRequest::get_direct`: validate scoped IDs, issue one cache-independent GET, exact-check the returned property ID, and discard embedded tags. Explicit-ID tag lookup separately scans at most 1,000 options and validates stable totals/complete windows before matching; `PropertyRequest::get` may prime every space property on a cold enabled cache.
[invariant] A keyed create may clear its idempotency entry only before the non-idempotent POST or after a definitive validation/auth rejection; timeout/cancellation/transport, malformed response, 5xx, or verification failure after dispatch must become a finite terminal conflict so retry can never issue a second POST.
[pattern] Concurrent create idempotency uses a short-held registry mutex plus a per-attempt result/Notify; all waiters share one verified result, and no registry lock is held over resolution, POST, GET, or result encoding.
[pattern] object_create fingerprints an explicit domain/version representation with presence markers after shared number/date/set/property normalization; idempotency keys themselves are never hashed into the request identity.
[invariant] Keyed create execution is owned by a detached supervised inner task, not the first caller; JoinError is completed according to shared MutationProgress so panic/abort wakes every waiter and retains only post-dispatch uncertainty.
[invariant] object_edit literal matching is ordered, left-to-right, and non-overlapping; old_text is nonempty, empty new_text deletes, and every hash/count/intermediate-size check completes before its single whole-body PATCH.
[gotcha] Anytype canonical Markdown GET bodies are not generally safe PATCH inputs: a literal underscore in the proven plain-line subset is returned as `\\_`, and replaying that body verbatim double-escapes it. Use `anytype::objects::plain_markdown_representation` to derive the unescaped wire form while hashing/verifying the exact canonical form; ambiguous Markdown stays byte-exact and fails closed on rewrites.
[invariant] The production MCP catalog is one sorted, startup-validated inventory built from the handlers' original typed contracts. Read-only mode omits every mutation and centrally rejects stale/direct mutation names before argument decoding, while each mutation handler retains its pre-I/O access gate as defense in depth. Static tool/resource capabilities never advertise list changes or subscriptions.
[pattern] Full normal/read-only MCP catalog fixtures live under `any-mcp/tests/snapshots`; ordinary CI only compares them. Contract changes require the explicit ignored updater documented beside the fixtures, followed by complete diff review—never environment-controlled auto-acceptance.
[invariant] The independent catalog schema audit must fail closed over the complete local-reference graph: only `#/$defs` refs, guarded cycle tracking, legal annotation siblings, explicit finite typed/const/enum/composition forms, and production string/array/numeric ceilings.
[gotcha] Live production-router tests must box `AnyMcpServer::dispatch_tool` before awaiting it; the aggregate debug future for all 14 branches otherwise overflows Rust's default test-thread stack. The boxed live suite passes on the default stack without `RUST_MIN_STACK`.
[pattern] Cleanup-owned live pagination fixtures use narrow test-only gRPC lifecycle helpers where the public REST surface is incomplete: disposable spaces are irreversibly deleted with bounded absence proof, and a second view is created on an owned collection/type and registered immediately for teardown.
[gotcha] Headless mutation diagnostics must query active and archived surfaces before `with_test_context` returns; otherwise teardown can manufacture archive evidence. Give acceptance fixtures a cleanup-owned unique type and filter active/archive evidence to it, avoiding assumptions about the shared space's archive size.
[invariant] `object_archive` preflights exact safe space/object/type identity, marks immediately before one `delete_once`, and requires bounded active absence plus original-type-scoped archived presence after every non-definitively-rejected dispatch; the DELETE body is never success evidence, and incomplete or unsafe evidence is mutation-indeterminate.
[transport] `object_archive` preflight, DELETE, and active-absence scan use HTTP; original-type-scoped archived presence uses `list_archived`, whose implementation unconditionally calls gRPC `object_search_with_meta`. HTTP-only operation cannot produce a verified archive success.
[invariant] Test-only non-idempotent fixture retries accept only typed `ApiError { code: 429 }`, reuse inputs computed before the closure, and register cleanup immediately after first success; asserted, optional-success, validation, transport, timeout, and 5xx mutations stay single-attempt.
[invariant] Cleanup-owned template fixtures derive source-snapshot capacity from their maximum 16 sources and scan only disjoint active/archived objects of the freshly proven custom type; unrelated long-lived space archives never gate ownership. Returned template IDs are registered immediately from the exact owned type/source relation and complete type-scoped pre-RPC absence, before response classification, then verified by bounded list/GET and unique-owner evidence. Teardown binds sources to that type and templates to their source, re-proves exact provenance before each destructive call, and skips all remaining cleanup for an owned type after any proof failure.
[gotcha] anyr is a binary crate with no lib target: run unit tests with `cargo test -p anyr --bins` (not --lib, which errors "no library targets").
[pattern] anyr clap parse tests reach the parser via `Cli::try_parse_from(["anyr", ...])` then match `cli.command` (Commands enum); missing-required-arg -> ErrorKind::MissingRequiredArgument, ArgGroup exclusivity -> ErrorKind::ArgumentConflict.
[pattern] anyr CLI handlers have no mock client; test decision logic by extracting pure helpers (e.g. property.rs choose_property_name/validate_property_update, types.rs TypePropertyMode/type_property_mode) rather than driving the async handler.
[invariant] anytype UpdateTypeRequest: .properties(iter) replaces the whole non-featured list, .clear_properties() sets it empty, omitting both leaves it unchanged; type update --add/--set/--clear map to these and are a clap mutually-exclusive ArgGroup.
[gotcha] anyr 'file' subcommand clap DECLARATIONS (FileCommands::Upload/Delete fields incl. --http, --file-type, and any new flag like --permanent) live in anyr/src/cli/mod.rs (~L266-377); only the HANDLER is in anyr/src/cli/file.rs. Any file-CLI flag add/remove/help-text change requires mod.rs edits.
[gotcha] A live any-mcp child and `with_test_context` cannot concurrently open the same file-backed db-keystore; production-process tests must give the child a test-owned temporary copy and remove it after child shutdown, without exposing credentials or adding a production test mode.
[pattern] MCP live coverage ownership is a typed one-owner manifest checked against the complete standard catalog and executable scenario IDs; the same discovery/document/view/mutation/archive scenarios run through direct-router and spawned-stdio drivers, while exhaustive protocol variants remain on scripted HTTP.
[gotcha] Fixture-heavy shared async scenario dispatch must return an erased heap-owned future and box large branches; retaining the aggregate Debug future inline can overflow Tokio's default test-worker stack before production behavior is observed.
[pattern] Keystore modifier boundaries are `:key=` with ASCII alphanumeric/underscore/hyphen keys, not every colon; this preserves Windows drive and ordinary colon-bearing paths. Isolation must retain ordered modifiers long enough to reject duplicate paths even though the runtime parser intentionally remains last-wins.
[invariant] Spawned live failure reports retain only fixed protocol categories and structural stderr byte/line/category counts. Raw diagnostic lines and unknown fields may be inspected transiently for classification but are never formatted or retained in the report.
[invariant] MCP startup transport admission is profile/access based: HTTP is universal, only standard read-write requires gRPC for object_archive verification, and any configured-but-unhealthy gRPC still fails every selection. Server construction rechecks the startup snapshot; catalogs are never dynamically filtered by availability.
